Progress status for this period: on_track. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your back is getting stronger — keep the momentum going this week.",
  "summary": "This was a strong period with a clear focus on your primary goal of building a bigger back. You moved over 10,700 kg of total tonnage, with more than half of all your training volume directed at the back — exactly what your goal calls for. Your back strength index jumped meaningfully, which is a great sign that the work is paying off. The rest of your body got some maintenance volume, and while it is on the lighter side for some groups, that trade-off is reasonable given your goal. Keep up the consistency and intent you showed this week.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "The back is the clear standout this period. Its strength index rose from 106 to 110, a gain of 4 points, which is well above the 3-point threshold that defines on_track progress. This is your most important lift category given your long-term goal, so this improvement is exactly what you want to see. Biceps held steady at 104, chest held at 100, shoulders held at 101, and legs held at 103 — none of these moved in either direction this period. Holding an index is not a problem for secondary muscle groups when your training volume is intentionally concentrated elsewhere, but it is worth keeping an eye on legs in particular given the 6 sets dedicated to them this week without a corresponding index increase.",
    "status": "on_track"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "chest",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Chest received only 2 sets this period, representing 8.9% of total volume. For a 28-year-old male at a healthy BMI, this is below what most balanced programs recommend for long-term structural health, even when back is the priority. It does not need to be a focus, but one or two additional sets per week would help maintain balance between pushing and pulling muscles."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders were also limited to 2 sets and 5.2% of total volume this period. Shoulder health is closely linked to both back and chest training, so keeping at least a small amount of direct shoulder work each week is worthwhile for joint integrity and long-term progress."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Maintain or slightly increase back volume — 12 sets worked well this week, and you could push toward 14 sets next period if recovery feels good, prioritizing compound movements like rows and pull-ups that drive the most hypertrophy.",
    "Add 1 to 2 sets each for chest and shoulders to protect pushing and pulling balance — even a couple of sets of overhead press or incline press is enough to keep these groups healthy without detracting from your back focus.",
    "Since your leg strength index did not move despite 6 sets of work, consider whether the load or intensity there needs a small nudge upward next period — a modest weight increase or an added set could get that index moving again.",
    "Continue monitoring your recovery between sessions — your tonnage per bodyweight ratio of 149.8 is solid for your profile, and the previous period's advice to watch recovery still applies as you push back volume higher."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Back strength index rose from 106 to 110 with 12 sets and 6,000 kg tonnage, confirming on_track progress toward the primary goal; all other muscle groups held steady with minimal volume, and mild push-pull imbalance in chest and shoulders is worth addressing next period."
}
